WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.573
In this video, I will demonstrate DenoiseAPT, an evidence-aware and
interactive system for time-series denoising.

2
00:00:09.073 --> 00:00:12.451
The interface connects automatic inference,

3
00:00:12.451 --> 00:00:20.331
configured anomaly evidence, reversible expert adaptation, and
provenance export in one local workflow.

4
00:00:20.331 --> 00:00:30.290
I will first use a rich CATS signal to show the complete process, and
then switch to a sparse MSL signal to highlight anomaly preservation.

5
00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.007
The interface has three stages.

6
00:00:35.707 --> 00:00:39.742
First, the user selects a
supported evaluation window.

7
00:00:39.742 --> 00:00:45.922
Second, the system compares denoising outputs and presents
preservation evidence.

8
00:00:45.922 --> 00:00:49.613
Third, the user can apply reversible expert input.

9
00:00:49.613 --> 00:00:52.704
Start and End define the fixed time window.

10
00:00:52.704 --> 00:01:00.086
The corruption controls reproduce a known evaluation condition and
are not part of normal inference.

11
00:01:00.586 --> 00:01:03.762
CSV upload is available only in review mode.

12
00:01:04.262 --> 00:01:07.438
I begin with CATS rich dynamics, using Start

13
00:01:07.438 --> 00:01:14.477
zero, End five hundred twelve, Gaussian corruption at zero point
seven five, and replicate zero.

14
00:01:14.677 --> 00:01:17.666
I then run the matched comparison.

15
00:01:19.566 --> 00:01:25.513
Every panel uses the same reference before corruption and the same
corrupted observation.

16
00:01:25.513 --> 00:01:28.680
The reference is available only for evaluation.

17
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:35.940
Median filtering, wavelet thresholding, Noisereduce, and the RINS-T
adaptation are frozen matched outputs.

18
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:43.391
Our Model follows the repeated rises, troughs,
and major transition while reducing the added corruption.

19
00:01:43.391 --> 00:01:50.806
For this condition, it has the lowest displayed error over both the
complete window and the labelled event region.

20
00:01:51.306 --> 00:01:54.009
This case illustrates the demonstration

21
00:01:54.009 --> 00:02:00.110
workflow, while the paper's aggregate conclusions come from the
complete evaluation panel.

22
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:07.380
I now select time points three hundred sixty-one through three
hundred seventy-five.

23
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:13.937
The evidence view compares the corrupted
observation with Our Model over the same interval.

24
00:02:13.937 --> 00:02:18.442
The teal band marks shared support under configured Scorer A. This is

25
00:02:18.442 --> 00:02:23.568
an inspection cue, not an anomaly probability or a
claim about physical truth.

26
00:02:24.068 --> 00:02:30.203
The automatic output also passes the
complete-window checks from configured Scorers A and B.

27
00:02:30.203 --> 00:02:35.018
The status card records the current state, check
result, and provenance.

28
00:02:35.518 --> 00:02:42.741
These evidence statements are intentionally
limited to the frozen scorers and thresholds used by the system.

29
00:02:44.341 --> 00:02:53.930
Next, I select time points three hundred seventy-nine through four
hundred eleven, where expert context can affect the preferred result.

30
00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:58.273
Observation weight beta changes only this selected interval.

31
00:02:58.273 --> 00:03:04.584
A value of zero keeps the automatic model output, while a value of
one keeps the observation.

32
00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:10.247
I move between both endpoints so the preview
clearly shows the difference.

33
00:03:10.247 --> 00:03:14.427
I then choose zero point seven five and apply the adaptation.

34
00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:20.254
The model parameters remain frozen, and only the
selected interval changes.

35
00:03:20.254 --> 00:03:25.581
The service recomputes the evidence and performs a
fresh full-window check.

36
00:03:26.081 --> 00:03:32.637
The adapted result still passes, and both
displayed errors improve in this illustrative edit.

37
00:03:33.137 --> 00:03:36.416
Revert removes the latest action, Restore Auto

38
00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:45.594
returns to the original model output, and Export Result records the
signals, settings, actions, checks, measures, and provenance.

39
00:03:47.294 --> 00:03:54.460
For a clearer preservation contrast, I switch to MSL anomaly
preservation and run its fixed impulse condition.

40
00:03:54.460 --> 00:04:00.406
This nearly flat sensor signal contains one important event at time
point two hundred sixty.

41
00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:06.013
At this event, the median filter removes the
peak, and RINS-T nearly removes it.

42
00:04:06.013 --> 00:04:09.138
Noisereduce retains only part of its amplitude.

43
00:04:09.638 --> 00:04:15.813
Wavelet thresholding also preserves the peak,
but leaves the surrounding impulse corruption.

44
00:04:16.313 --> 00:04:19.286
Our Model preserves almost the complete event

45
00:04:19.286 --> 00:04:28.052
while suppressing the injected impulses, and it has the lowest
displayed complete-window and event-region errors for this condition.

46
00:04:28.052 --> 00:04:34.836
This shows the important balance between denoising and preserving
short, meaningful signal structure.

47
00:04:36.436 --> 00:04:43.786
Configured Scorers A and B both cross near time point two hundred
sixty for the corrupted observation and Our Model.

48
00:04:44.286 --> 00:04:47.393
The complete-window check passes, showing that

49
00:04:47.393 --> 00:04:54.364
the preserved peak remains supported without adding a new detected
interval under these configured checks.

50
00:04:54.864 --> 00:04:58.122
Together, the two cases demonstrate synchronized

51
00:04:58.122 --> 00:05:02.062
denoising comparison, inspectable preservation evidence,

52
00:05:02.062 --> 00:05:08.124
expert-guided adaptation, reversible checking, and auditable export
in one local workflow.

53
00:05:08.924 --> 00:05:10.439
Thank you for watching.
